// src/v4/lens/application.dag
//
// Scope: lens-application surface — how a user attaches a lens to a program section (declaration / module / expression scope) with configurable enforcement. THE opt-in-depth mechanism + user-defined-dimensions surface + the ONLY advisory→fail-closed bridge. Referenced by report.dag/synthesis.dag's apply_lens(Enforce) — this file is its substrate home.
// Anchor: THESIS:95-101 (user-defined dimensions) + THESIS:307-321 (audience duality / opt-in depth) + r3-structure.md:40 (v3 T-Lens-Application-Surface precedent)
//
// Owns:
//   - EnforcedApplication<Output, Budget, Projected> (enforceable_lens, section,
//     budget, diagnostic_severity, span)
//   - IntrospectApplication<Output> (lens, section, span; no Budget — advisory)
//   - SectionRef = DeclarationScope | NodeScope (disjoint sum; where the lens attaches)
//   - apply_lens(lens, section, config) — the user-facing surface
//   - THE single advisory→fail-closed bridge: Enforce { } converts a lens's
//     Set<Report> into fail-closed Diagnostics (per std/report.dag discipline);
//     no other path from advisory to blocking exists
//   - D1 OPERATIONS over the std/node.dag structural-delta types
//     (Path/Edit/Diff — the DATA lives in std/node.dag):
//       - subterm_at(root: Node, p: Path) -> Result<Node, Diagnostic>
//         — the subtree at a Path (term-rewriting `t|p`); a Path that
//         does not resolve ⇒ fail-closed Diagnostic, never a guess
//       - apply_diff(root: Node, d: Diff) -> Result<Node, Diagnostic>
//         — fold the Diff's ORDERED Edits as SEQUENTIAL rewrites
//         (`t[s]p` per Edit); all-or-nothing (any Edit whose Path no
//         longer resolves ⇒ one Diagnostic, no partial application).
//         NO independence/well-formedness predicate — any List<Edit>
//         is a valid rewrite program (P2 finding 2, PR #3162).
//         Consumer = CLI / test harness / user application code.
//
// QRY-1 (operator-corrected 2026-05-15) — there is NO "query" concept
// and NO user-vs-kernel distinction. The earlier "query = user-defined
// lens" framing is RETRACTED: user-authored vs kernel-shipped is
// PROVENANCE, not a structural distinction — the compiler sees through
// who wrote a lens (feedback_naming_is_aliasing). A lens is a lens. A
// name distinguished only by who-authored-it is decorative ⇒ it must not
// exist (operator: "a real distinction, or not exist").
//
// The REAL axis the operator surfaced ("a filter on a lens") is
// SCOPE / PARAMETERIZATION — and it is already modeled, two ways, with
// NO new noun:
//   1. SectionRef = DeclarationScope | NodeScope (below) — applying a
//      lens AT a scope (a sub-Node) IS a scoped read. "ask <lens> about
//      <this section>" is `apply_lens(lens, NodeScope(x), Introspect)`.
//   2. A lens signature may TAKE a parameter. The archetype is the
//      affected-set lens (T-21): `affected_set:(Dag,Diff)->Witness<
//      ReExecFrontier>`. "the affected set of THIS change" is exactly
//      `affected_set(dag, diff)` — a lens whose answer is relative to a
//      parameter, not absolute. Already first-class, not a query system.
// So: lenses, read at scopes and/or parameterized. No `query.dag`, no
// query subsystem, no authorship split — those would be the decorative
// parallel authority the closed system forbids (STOP). The B2-OMNI
// Node-pivot "one read, all ingested languages" property still holds —
// it is a property of LENSES (any lens over Node), not of a "query".
//
// D1 (operator-ratified 2026-05-15; shape refined per a P2 review
// finding, PR #3162) — this file is the OPERATION home for the
// structural-delta vocabulary whose DATA TYPES (Path/Edit/Diff) live
// in std/node.dag. The split is FORCED, not stylistic:
// std/diagnostic.dag already `Consumes std/node.dag`
// (`Correction = Suggested(Node)`), so a node.dag operation returning
// `Result<_, Diagnostic>` would be a substrate cycle. subterm_at/
// apply_diff are exactly the "consumers evaluate fail-closed and raise
// the Diagnostic" discipline std/node.dag states for `well_formed`.
// apply_diff applies the Diff's ORDERED Edits SEQUENTIALLY — there is
// NO independence/well-formedness predicate (any List<Edit> is valid;
// the prior set+predicate shape was the P2 illegal-states anti-pattern).
// These ops are part of this file's T-23 fill; the I/O contract in
// this header is immutable.
//
// AGENT-SURFACE (operator-ratified 2026-05-15) — designed early so it
// has a structural PLACE, not bolted on later; "a very important /
// exclusive feature" but a NATURAL CONSEQUENCE of already-ratified
// pieces, NOT a new subsystem (and NOT the north star — included,
// designed properly, placed). A non-text client (an LLM/agent)
// interacts with the program AS DATA, never as files/text:
//   READ  — it does not grep/read files; it READS LENSES at a scope:
//           `apply_lens(lens, SectionRef, Introspect)` (this file) +
//           the affected-set lens `affected_set(dag, diff)` (T-21,
//           lens/affected_set.dag). The affected lens IS what replaces
//           file-reading exploration ("what is even relevant here").
//   WRITE — it does not emit a text patch; a change is the **D1**
//           `apply_diff : (Node, Diff) -> Result<Node, Diagnostic>`
//           (the ratified D1 operation in this file — NOT a total
//           `-> Node`). An agent-supplied Diff is EXTERNAL input that
//           may be invalid or stale, so the boundary is FAIL-CLOSED
//           (INVARIANTS P3): an unresolved/ill-formed Diff ⇒ a typed
//           Diagnostic, all-or-nothing, never a total transform.
//           AGENT-1 USES D1's apply_diff; it does not restate a total
//           one. The change is ALREADY Node, so it does NOT pass
//           `ingest` (no source text, no LanguageModel, no parse): it
//           enters the B2-OMNI pipeline at `core` — a SIBLING entry to
//           `ingest`, not an instance of it: `emit ∘ core ∘ apply_diff`
//           (cf. `compile = emit ∘ core ∘ ingest`). Diff
//           content-addressed by B1 `content_hash` — identity over
//           structure, not lines.
//   BACK  — it gets the `affected_set` lens read — the minimal
//           re-execution cone of its change — in whatever shape T-21 /
//           lens/affected_set.dag DECLARES. AGENT-1 is a CLIENT: it
//           defers to that lens as the single authority (P2) and coins
//           NO return noun of its own (no `Witness<ReExecFrontier>` or
//           any other AGENT-1-introduced type — that ungrounded noun is
//           what this corrects, P1/P2). Plus a FAITHFUL re-emit:
//           node-level (C5-1 `ingest∘emit = id_Node`, hash-checked) +
//           emit-locality (C5-2) + C4 committed == emit — no lossy text
//           round-trip.
//   GATE  — gated like any other change: the relevant lenses run and
//           `apply_lens(_, Enforce)` (the single advisory→fail-closed
//           bridge above) blocks fail-closed. The agent earns no
//           special trust; structure gates it.
// BOUNDARY: this is a CLIENT of (apply_lens here) + (affected_set T-21)
// + (the B2-OMNI pipeline, entered at `core` via apply_diff, then
// `emit`) + (C5/C4 for the faithful re-emit). It introduces NO new
// authority,
// noun, or query subsystem (consistent with QRY-1 above +
// feedback_closed_system_design). It is the ratified COMPOSITION named
// and given a home, so it is a contract T-23 is accountable to — not an
// accident waiting to be true. Honest status: this is the DESIGN; the
// surface is realized only when T-23 composes T-21 + B2-OMNI + C5/C4
// (all scaffold today — nothing runs yet).
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag (incl. the D1 Path/Edit/Diff types),
//     std/witness.dag, std/diagnostic.dag, std/report.dag
//   - lens/*.dag: the lenses being applied (complexity/cost/synthesis/...)
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-23
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).
// See (T-23-PIN).

module v4.lens.application

